mlterm-fb embed mode

* What this fork is

  This is a downstream fork of arakiken/mlterm that adds a "render to
  a host-supplied buffer" mode to the framebuffer (`--with-gui=fb`)
  backend. Upstream's fb backend opens /dev/fb0 and mmap()s the
  kernel framebuffer; this fork extracts the buffer ownership behind
  a small interface so the same rendering pipeline can drive a memory
  buffer supplied by an embedding host (e.g. via JNI, a coroutine
  driver, or another runtime).

  Sixel and ReGIS support carry over unchanged — they're rendered by
  the same uitoolkit/fb code path, so they "just work" against the
  buffer-target backend the same way they work against /dev/fb0.

  The fork's policy is to stay rebaseable on top of upstream/master.
  Every commit on the `fb-embed` topic branch is one focused change
  with a clear message; resyncing means `git fetch upstream && git
  rebase upstream/master` and resolving any conflicts in the small
  number of files we touch.

* Scope

  Things this fork DOES change:
    - uitoolkit/fb/ui_display.c — the chokepoint where /dev/fb0 is
      opened and mmap'd. Refactored so the actual buffer can come
      from a host via a registered backend.
    - uitoolkit/fb/ui_event_source.c — the loop that reads
      /dev/input/eventN. Made externally drivable so a host can push
      key/mouse events without owning the file descriptors.
    - configure / configure.in — adds --enable-fb-embed (off by
      default; building this on disables device-node IO and exposes
      the embed entry points instead).
    - A small embed-API header (uitoolkit/fb/ui_fb_embed.h) declaring
      the backend-registration and event-injection functions a host
      uses.

  Things this fork DOES NOT change:
    - The VT parser (vtemu/) — completely backend-agnostic upstream,
      no changes needed.
    - Sixel / ReGIS handling — already factored cleanly upstream.
    - Other backends (X11, Cocoa, Win32, SDL, Wayland, …) — not
      touched, not tested, may bitrot if they take incidental damage
      from header reorganization. Pure framebuffer-embed focus.
    - mlterm's binary (`mlterm-fb`) — still builds and still works
      against /dev/fb0 when --enable-fb-embed is OFF.

* Build (embed mode, for hosts)

    ./configure --with-gui=fb --enable-fb-embed --disable-anti-alias \
                --disable-fontconfig --disable-otl
    make

  Output: libmlterm-fb-embed.a (static lib) plus the embed-API
  header. No `mlterm-fb` binary is built in embed mode.

* Build (regular fb, for testing rebases)

    ./configure --with-gui=fb
    make

  Same as upstream. Useful to confirm that rebasing onto a new
  upstream commit didn't break the device-node path.

* Upstream relationship

  Upstream is github.com/arakiken/mlterm. We track its `master`
  branch on the `upstream` remote in this repo. Our `master` branch
  is a literal mirror of upstream/master; never commit to it
  directly. All work goes on `fb-embed`. To sync:

    git fetch upstream
    git checkout master && git merge --ff-only upstream/master
    git push fork master
    git checkout fb-embed && git rebase master

  The conflict surface is tiny by design — most patches are in
  files upstream rarely touches.

* Embedding host

  This fork is consumed by Scalar Evolution
  (github.com/LekKit/ScalarEvolution), a Minecraft mod that runs
  RISC-V VMs in-world and wants real terminal rendering for its VT100
  block. The host-side build harness lives at native/mlterm-jni/ in
  that repo; see its PORT_PLAN.md for the integration shape.
